+# FAQ for newcomers
+
+## How do I install the command line tool?
+
+Install it with pipx (Python 3.11 or newer): `pipx install tally-toolkit`. If it is not found, run
+`pipx ensurepath` and open a new terminal. Check it with `tallyctl version`.
+
+## What is the tool called now?
+
+`tallyctl`. It was called `tally-cli` until the 2026-09 release; the old name works as an alias for one more release
+and will then be removed.
+
+## Which port does tally listen on?
+
+Port 8731, since the 2026-09 release. The old port 8080 is closed; anything still using it gets a refused connection.
+
+## How do I clear the cache?
+
+Run `tallyctl cache clear`. It clears all regions; add `--region NAME` to clear only one. The old `purge-cache`
+command was removed.
+
+## How long until an unanswered page escalates?
+
+15 minutes without acknowledgement (cut from 30 on 16 September 2026). The page goes from the primary
+on-call to the secondary, then to the engineering manager.
+
+## Does the pager wake me at night?
+
+Only for severity 1: customer-facing data loss or a full outage. Between 22:00 and 07:00, severity 2 incidents, such as
+a slow dashboard or a delayed batch, do not page; the primary picks them up in the morning.
+
+## What do I do when the ingest queue is stuck?
+
+Restart ingest with a drain first and look for the cause afterwards: `tallyctl restart ingest --drain`. It finishes
+in-flight work, parks the stuck batch in the dead-letter table and starts the consumer clean. A plain restart
+reloads the bad batch.
+
+## What do I do when the API certificate has expired?
+
+Renew it, then restart the API, which only serves the new certificate after a restart: `tallyctl certs renew api`,
+then `tallyctl restart api`. `tallyctl certs list` shows expiry dates.
